 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public housing law, in relation to the powers of the\ndivision of housing and community renewal",
    "basePrintNo": "S6412",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6412-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §14, Pub Hous L",
    "lawSection": "Public Housing Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6412\n\nSPONSOR: KAVANAGH\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the public housing law, in relation to\nthe powers of the division of housing and community renewal\n\n \nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nThis bill would amend the Public Housing Law to allow the Division of\nHousing and Community Renewal (DHCR), the Housing Finance Agency, and\nHousing Trust Fund Corporation and its subsidiaries (collectively \"HCR\")\nto impose penalties and other remedies necessary to correct violations\nof laws, regulations, or directives by housing providers that receive\nfinancing or other benefits from HCR. This bill is necessary to ensure\ncompliance with the various requirements of affordable housing programs,\nthereby, providing programmatic efficiencies and best use of HCR's\nspending.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThe bill amends Section 14 of the Public Housing Law by adding a new\nsubdivision 8, as follows:\n\nSection 1 provides that the Division of Housing and Community Renewal,\nthe Housing Finance Agency and the Housing Trust Fund Corporation and\nits subsidiaries, are authorized to:\n\n  A. Issue and impose orders, directives, and assess actual and pecuni-\n     ary penalties, or other remedies necessary to ensure compliance and\n     penalize noncompliance with law, regulation, directive, or agree-\n     ment;\n  B. Establish the procedure for the implementation of the requirements\n     of the amendment; and\n  C. Promulgate regulations and rules necessary to effectuate this\n     amendment. The regulations and rules promulgated shall be operable\n     to all housing providers, housing projects, or other entities that\n     receive financing from HCR.\n\nSection 2 provides for an immediate effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIt is in the state's interest to ensure that the affordable housing\nproviders that receive financing from HCR comply the law and regulatory\nagreements. The legislature created, as did the various housing agen-\ncies, a variety of funding mechanisms and programs to provide affordable\nhousing, as well as remedies for failure to provide what is required by\nlaw, regulation or directive. In light of the various funding incentives\nand affordable housing schemes, the programs and laws must in some ways\ndiffer. However, both the state and housing developers will benefit from\na more standard and unified approach, where possible, to procure similar\nlaws and procedures for noncompliance. In addition, the bill's require-\nments that the program remedies would be created by regulation or other\nprocedures, has the benefit that recipients and potential recipient of\nHCR financing will be able to comment and interact during the process.\n\nThe passage of the bill will assist compliance efforts by allowing HCR\nto issue orders, directive and impose actual and pecuniary penalties for\nviolations committed by affordable housing providers. Additionally,\ncodifying this power means that housing providers will comply with their\nlegal obligations to tenants if they receive financing from HCR. This\nprotects tenants and stabilizes the relation ship between the housing\nprovider and tenants, creating stronger and safer communities. The bill\nis necessary in facilitating HCR's ability to provide and preserve\naffordable housing across the state.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNone\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nThere is no anticipated budget impact on this proposed legislation. In\nfact, this bill will help maintain and provide funds for the state.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediate\n",
